{
  "summary": "This computer looks in good shape: the scan found only three minor items, none of them urgent. Two are changes since last week's scan that look like routine software updates, and one is a reminder to confirm you recognize an administrator account. The first thing worth a quick look is that administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "An extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account named <computer>\\<account-2> with full administrator rights. Having a separate admin account is actually good practice, and this account was not newly added since the last scan (new admins would have been flagged at high). Still, an admin account can change anything on the computer, so just confirm it's one you or someone in your household created. If you recognize it, nothing more is needed; if not, see the fix shown in the report for this finding."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Microsoft Defender's service now points to a newer version",
      "finding_ids": [
        "CHANGED-PERSISTENCE:47c86358"
      ],
      "explanation": "The auto-start entry for Windows Defender (your built-in antivirus) changed since the scan 7 days ago. The only difference is the version folder in the path, from 4.18.25010-1 to 4.18.25020-2, still inside Microsoft's Windows Defender folder. That matches what a normal Defender platform update looks like. The rule flags these changes because redirecting a trusted entry is a known trick, but this one looks like routine updating."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "New Google updater scheduled task",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-PERSISTENCE:c74ac833"
      ],
      "explanation": "A new scheduled task called \\GoogleUpdaterTaskSystem appeared since last week. It runs Google's updater from the Program Files (x86)\\Google folder, which is what Google software such as Chrome installs to keep itself up to date. If you have Google software installed or recently installed it, this is expected and harmless. It's listed for information only."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
